WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.747
Last video for week four.

2
00:00:01.797 --> 00:00:13.248
Twenty cases times three runs is sixty traces, and some of your checks cannot be a rule: is this summary faithful, is this email appropriate, did the agent explain the refusal politely.

3
00:00:13.298 --> 00:00:16.842
For those you will use a model as a judge.

4
00:00:16.892 --> 00:00:19.358
This video is about doing that honestly.

5
00:00:20.108 --> 00:00:22.298
A judge is a second model call.

6
00:00:22.348 --> 00:00:31.261
It reads the task, the agent's output, and often the trace, and it answers a narrow question against a rubric you wrote: pass or fail, with a reason.

7
00:00:31.311 --> 00:00:32.222
Keep it binary.

8
00:00:32.272 --> 00:00:37.663
Scores out of ten drift from run to run, and nobody can say what a seven means.

9
00:00:37.713 --> 00:00:42.465
Do not let the model grade itself without a rubric; models prefer their own style.

10
00:00:42.515 --> 00:00:45.666
And a judge is not a replacement for reading traces.

11
00:00:45.716 --> 00:00:49.508
It is a way to scale what you learned from reading them.

12
00:00:50.257 --> 00:00:53.257
Model judges have known biases, documented since 2023.

13
00:00:53.307 --> 00:00:58.975
Position bias: in a comparison, the judge prefers whichever answer it saw first, or last.

14
00:00:59.025 --> 00:01:01.643
Length bias: it prefers the longer answer.

15
00:01:01.693 --> 00:01:08.886
Self-preference: it prefers text in its own style, which matters if the agent and the judge share a model.

16
00:01:08.936 --> 00:01:11.554
And it believes a confident wrong answer.

17
00:01:11.604 --> 00:01:26.420
Each of these can be measured, and each has a fix: randomize the order, grade one answer at a time against a rubric rather than comparing, use a different model family, and ask for the evidence before the verdict.

18
00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:31.091
Calibration is how you earn the right to use a judge.

19
00:01:31.141 --> 00:01:38.311
Take thirty to fifty traces and label them yourselves, pass or fail, with two people labeling independently and resolving disagreements.

20
00:01:38.361 --> 00:01:42.281
Write the rubric and run the judge on the same traces.

21
00:01:42.331 --> 00:01:43.003
Then compare.

22
00:01:43.053 --> 00:01:46.613
Where the judge and the humans disagree, read those cases.

23
00:01:46.663 --> 00:01:48.418
Usually the rubric was vague.

24
00:01:48.468 --> 00:01:50.223
Sometimes the humans were wrong.

25
00:01:50.273 --> 00:01:52.389
Fix the rubric and run again.

26
00:01:52.439 --> 00:02:01.774
You are done when agreement is high enough that you would act on the judge's verdict, and you report that agreement number in the evaluation report.

27
00:02:01.824 --> 00:02:06.106
A judge nobody calibrated is an opinion with a cost per call.

28
00:02:06.856 --> 00:02:23.052
So in the evaluation report, include the rubric verbatim, the agreement between the judge and the human labels and on how many cases it was measured, the kinds of failure the judge tends to miss, and which model judged and whether it shares a family with the agent.

29
00:02:23.102 --> 00:02:28.128
Readers of your report will trust the judge exactly as much as that section earns.

30
00:02:28.178 --> 00:02:29.821
Next week we change sides.

31
00:02:29.871 --> 00:02:35.574
You will attack your own agent, and learn why the fixes are architecture rather than better prompts.
